- There are many purposes for which one would want a fileset
    - To analyse something stand-alone, as a 'module'
    - a 'vertical slice' through the layers (ingestion/validation for the purpose of a particular entity)
    - the essentails of the system , for readme work
    - bragpacks
Approaches:
- Deterministic
    - repo type specific
        - code? which language?
        - technically hard, huge rabbithole
        - .. I think?
- HIL LLM based:
    - To recruit we must present
    - so file metadata + current set of filesets. attempt to 'eyeball it'
    - then QA filesets via LLM? 'does this set provide complete ctx for <suchandso> concern?'
- User history based
    - Suppose the app generates a history of used filesets
    - This could provide some value?
Additional concerns & thoughts:
- token economy always matters
- there are upper bounds to context size
- the human should not have to manually edit yaml.
    - that is horrible
    - and clankerwork

IX: Standard 3-Stage Prompt Payload Layout

To maximize LLM retrieval accuracy and enforce strict output compliance across all modes, the render pipeline must assemble prompt payloads into a deterministic 3-stage sequence:

1. Rules & Execution Framework (Top)
   - Establish operational persona, system directives, and behavioural rules
   - Assets: general-rules.md, behavioural_instructions/ (e.g. plan-mode.md, do-mode.md), doctrines/
   - Purpose: Sets the primary attention mask through which all subsequent data is interpreted

2. Passive Payload & Grounding Data (Middle)
   - Contain all raw reference artifacts, project docs, and current state payloads
   - Assets: <repo-content> (Filesets), <backlog.cdoc>, <project-history.cdoc>
   - Purpose: Functions as a passive lookup pool; placed in the middle attention zone to prevent context contamination

3. Output Instructions & Mandates (Bottom)
   - Define exact syntax, structural rules, formatting specs, and negative constraints
   - Assets: output-instructions/ (e.g. backlog-output-instructions.md, code-output-instruction.md)
   - Purpose: Maximizes recency attention weight directly adjacent to token generation